%[text] # Part 3 - HDL Cosimulation: Run Your Verilog Inside Simulink
%[text] **Goal:** verify the hand-written `fir4_legacy.v` against the Part 1 Simulink model, bit for bit, using the Simulink test bench you already have - no SystemVerilog testbench to write.
%[text] With HDL cosimulation, Simulink generates the stimulus and checks the results while an HDL simulator executes the RTL. HDL Verifier keeps the two in lock step: every Simulink step, inputs are forced into the simulator, the simulator advances by one clock, and outputs come back. Supported simulators include **Vivado Simulator** (used here - it ships with Vivado, no extra license), Siemens Questa/ModelSim, Cadence Xcelium and Synopsys VCS.
%[text] Typical uses: proving legacy RTL matches the model (this part), verifying HDL Coder output, mixing RTL blocks with Simulink blocks, and simulating the real RTL inside the Part 2 black box.
%[text] **Version note:** with MATLAB R2026a use **Vivado 2024\.1**. The wizard accepts 2025\.1 but warns that it "has not been fully tested with the current release".
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Contents"}
%%
%[text] ## 1. Start the Cosimulation Wizard
%[text] The wizard writes its files (compile scripts, the compiled simulator library, a new model) into the current folder, so start in a work folder:
demoRoot = fileparts(matlab.desktop.editor.getActiveFilename);
workDir = fullfile(demoRoot, "cosim_work");
if ~isfolder(workDir), mkdir(workDir); end
cd(workDir)
%[text] Then **Apps** tab > **HDL Verifier** > **Cosimulation Wizard** (or type `cosimWizard`).
%[text] **Cosimulation Type** - HDL Simulator `Vivado Simulator`, cosimulation with `Simulink`. Point it at the Vivado `bin` folder if Vivado is not on the system path.
%[text] ![Cosimulation Type](text:image:9bae01)
%%
%[text] ## 2. Add the HDL files
%[text] Click **Add** and select `hdl_src/fir4_legacy.v`. The file type is detected automatically; for several files the compile order is worked out by the simulator.
%[text] ![HDL Files](text:image:acc202)
%[text] **HDL Compilation** - the wizard shows the Vivado Tcl it will use (create a project, add files, elaborate). Defaults are fine. Click **Next**: Vivado compiles and elaborates your RTL (about 30-60 s).
%[text] ![HDL Compilation](text:image:f40703)
%%
%[text] ## 3. Choose the top module
%[text] **Simulation Options** - the top module `fir4_legacy` is found automatically from the elaborated design.
%[text] ![Simulation Options](text:image:666e04)
%[text] **Input/Output Ports** - the wizard read the module ports and guessed their roles: `clk` = **Clock**, `rst` = **Reset**, `clk_en` and `x_in` = **Input**, `y_out` = **Output**. Clock and reset are driven by the simulator itself (Tcl force commands), so they will not appear as Simulink ports.
%[text] ![Input/Output Ports](text:image:fd1c05)
%%
%[text] ## 4. Give the output a meaning
%[text] **Output Port Details** - again, Verilog has no binary point. Tell Simulink how to interpret `y_out`: Data Type `Fixedpoint`, Sign `Signed`, Fraction Length `18` (Q4\.18, the same type as the Part 1 output). Sample time `1` = one Simulink step.
%[text] ![Output Port Details](text:image:c99a06)
%%
%[text] ## 5. Clock, reset and time alignment
%[text] **Clock/Reset Details** - `clk` period 10 ns, rising edge; `rst` active high for the first 8 ns. This is the part of your old testbench that the simulator still owns.
%[text] ![Clock/Reset Details](text:image:691507)
%[text] **Start Time Alignment** - the red line is where Simulink starts driving inputs: after reset has been applied at the first rising edge and away from any clock edge (no race).
%[text] ![Start Time Alignment](text:image:fd6208)
%%
%[text] ## 6. Generate the cosimulation block
%[text] **Block Generation** - untick *Automatically determine timescale* and set **1 second in Simulink = 10 ns in the HDL simulator**. One Simulink step (1 s) is now exactly one 10 ns clock. Click **Finish**.
%[text] ![Block Generation](text:image:12d209)
%[text] The wizard opens a new model with the **HDL Cosimulation** block for `fir4_legacy` (ports `clk_en`, `x_in`, `y_out`) and a **Generate DLL** helper: double-click it whenever the Verilog changes to recompile.
%[text] ![Generated HDL Cosimulation block](text:image:7eec10)
%%
%[text] ## 7. Reuse the Part 1 test bench
%[text] Copy the HDL Cosimulation block into a test bench with the same stimulus as Part 1, feed `clk_en` with a Constant `1` (boolean), and compare against the Part 1 `FIR_Filter` delayed by one Unit Delay (the Verilog registers its output). The finished version is `cosim_work/fir_cosim_tb.slx`:
open_system(fullfile(workDir, "fir_cosim_tb.slx"))
%[text] ![Cosimulation test bench](text:image:beaa11)
%[text] Click **Run**. Simulink and xsim run together; the Scope shows the RTL output, the golden output and their difference:
out = sim("fir_cosim_tb");
maxError = max(abs(double(out.logsout.get("err").Values.Data)))
%[text] ![RTL vs golden model](text:image:bfe412)
%[text] `err` is identically zero: the hand-written Verilog is bit-exact with the Simulink model for this stimulus.
%%
%[text] ## 8. Try it: catch a bug
%[text] 1. Edit `hdl_src/fir4_legacy.v` and change tap 2 from `(x_d2 <<< 1) + x_d2` to `(x_d2 <<< 1)` - a typical shift-and-add slip.
%[text] 2. Double-click **Generate DLL** in the model to recompile the RTL.
%[text] 3. Run again: `err` is no longer zero and the Scope shows exactly when and by how much the RTL is wrong. Undo the change afterwards \
%[text] Swap the stimulus for a chirp, random noise or recorded data and the same test bench re-verifies the RTL in seconds.
%%
%[text] ## 9. Can I keep my Verilog testbench?
%[text] Yes. Sections 1-8 made Simulink the test bench. If you already have Verilog / SystemVerilog testbenches, there are three ways to keep them, depending on who is in charge:
%[text:table]
%[text] |  | **A. Verilog TB drives, Simulink checks** | **B. Simulink model exported into your TB (DPI-C)** | **C. Verilog TB calls MATLAB (matlabcp / matlabtb)** |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | Who generates stimulus | Your Verilog TB, unchanged | Your SV TB / UVM environment | Your Verilog TB |
%[text] | Where the golden model runs | Simulink, alongside the simulator | Inside the HDL simulator, as compiled C | MATLAB function, called from HDL |
%[text] | Simulink/MATLAB running? | Yes (cosimulation) | No - stand-alone in the simulator | Yes (MATLAB) |
%[text] | Vivado Simulator | Yes - shown below | Yes, with the *ASIC Testbench for HDL Verifier* add-on | No - Questa/ModelSim or Xcelium only |
%[text] | Best for | Reusing existing TBs right now, debugging with Simulink scopes | Regression suites and UVM scoreboards; nightly runs without MATLAB | Algorithm checks inside an existing Questa/Xcelium flow |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] ### 9A. Verilog testbench as stimulus, Simulink as the checker
%[text] `hdl_src/tb_fir4_legacy.v` is a typical self-contained testbench: no ports; it generates its own clock and reset, applies a noisy square wave with `$random`, prints with `$display`, and ends with `$finish`. We use it **unchanged**.
%[text] ```verilog
%[text] module tb_fir4_legacy;
%[text]     reg clk = 1'b0;  reg rst = 1'b1;  reg clk_en = 1'b1;
%[text]     reg  signed [15:0] x_in = 16'sd0;
%[text]     wire signed [18:0] y_out;
%[text]     fir4_legacy dut (.clk(clk), .rst(rst), .clk_en(clk_en), .x_in(x_in), .y_out(y_out));
%[text]     always #5 clk = ~clk;
%[text]     initial begin repeat (3) @(posedge clk); rst <= 1'b0; end
%[text]     always @(posedge clk)
%[text]         if (!rst) begin
%[text]             n    <= n + 1;
%[text]             x_in <= (((n % 64) < 32) ? 16'sd12000 : -16'sd12000) + ($random(seed) % 4000);
%[text]         end
%[text] `ifndef COSIM
%[text]     initial #5000 $finish;      // stand-alone run length
%[text] `endif
%[text] endmodule
%[text] ```
%[text] The Vivado Simulator cosimulation connects to **top-level ports only**, so we add a 5-line wrapper whose outputs are hierarchical references into the TB. Nothing in the TB or the DUT changes:
%[text] ```verilog
%[text] module tb_cosim_monitor (output signed [15:0] x_mon, output signed [18:0] y_mon);
%[text]     tb_fir4_legacy tb ();          // your testbench, instantiated as-is
%[text]     assign x_mon = tb.x_in;
%[text]     assign y_mon = tb.y_out;
%[text] endmodule
%[text] ```
%[text] (With Questa or Xcelium you can skip the wrapper: the HDL Cosimulation block can connect to internal signals such as `/tb_fir4_legacy/y_out` directly.)
%[text] **Cosimulation Wizard, same as before, with three differences:**
%[text] **(1)** **HDL Files**: add the DUT, the testbench and the wrapper.
%[text] ![HDL files: DUT + testbench + wrapper](text:image:76d613)
%[text] **(2)** **HDL Compilation**: add two lines to the generated Tcl. One defines `COSIM`, so the TB's `$finish` is skipped and Simulink decides when to stop; the other sets the wrapper as the top.
%[text] ```
%[text] set_property verilog_define {COSIM} [get_filesets sim_1]
%[text] set_property top tb_cosim_monitor [get_filesets sim_1]
%[text] ```
%[text] ![Edited compilation commands](text:image:d95314)
%[text] **(3)** **Ports**: the top has **outputs only**. There is no clock or reset to force, because the TB makes its own. The wizard therefore skips the Clock/Reset and Start Time pages. Set `x_mon` to signed with fraction length 15 and `y_mon` to signed with fraction length 18, and on the last page use the same timescale (1 s in Simulink = 10 ns, one clock).
%[text] ![Outputs only](text:image:cb4815)
%[text] ![Output types](text:image:22f116)
%[text] Then feed the observed stimulus `x_mon` into the golden model and compare it with the observed response `y_mon`. The model is `cosim_tb_work/fir_vtb_check.slx`:
open_system(fullfile(demoRoot, "cosim_tb_work", "fir_vtb_check.slx"))
out = sim("fir_vtb_check");
maxError = max(abs(double(out.logsout.get("err").Values.Data)))
%[text] ![Verilog TB drives, Simulink checks](text:image:8a9617)
%[text] ![Stimulus from the Verilog TB, DUT response, and zero error](text:image:f5d618)
%[text] Your testbench keeps doing what it always did. Simulink adds a golden-model check with zero effort on the RTL side, and you get Simulink scopes and the Data Inspector to debug any mismatch.
%%
%[text] ### 9B. Export the golden model into your testbench (SystemVerilog DPI)
%[text] HDL Verifier can turn `FIR_Filter` (or any subsystem or MATLAB function) into a **SystemVerilog DPI component**: C code plus an SV wrapper module that your testbench instantiates like any other module. It runs in Vivado Simulator, Questa, Xcelium or VCS **without MATLAB**, which makes it the natural fit for UVM scoreboards and nightly regressions. In Simulink: **Configuration Parameters** > **Code Generation** > *System target file* = `systemverilog_dpi_grt.tlc`, pick your simulator under **SystemVerilog DPI**, then right-click the subsystem > **C/C++ Code** > **Build This Subsystem**.
%[text] This requires the **ASIC Testbench for HDL Verifier** add-on, which is not installed on this demo machine, so this option is described here but not run.
%[text] ### 9C. Your testbench calls MATLAB
%[text] With Questa/ModelSim or Xcelium, `matlabcp` (MATLAB as a component) and `matlabtb` (MATLAB as a test bench) let a Verilog TB call a MATLAB function every clock or on an event, so the TB stays the master. This workflow is **not supported with Vivado Simulator**.
%%
%[text] ## Combining with Part 2
%[text] Put this HDL Cosimulation block inside the Part 2 BlackBox subsystem instead of the behavioral model. HDL Coder still instantiates `fir4_legacy` in the generated code, and Simulink now simulates the real RTL - one model, no gap between what you simulate and what you build.
%[text] **Next:** Part 4 takes the verified design onto a real FPGA board.

